{#
	The numbering pattern, and the number it will produce (XIV-27).

	Every block below is derived from the *typed* pattern rather than from the
	stored one, which is the point: the preview, the counter it names and the
	warning about switching counters all have to be true of what is in the box
	before anybody presses Save. The stored pattern is consulted for exactly one
	question — `this.changingCounter`, whether the counter is about to change —
	because that is the only statement here that is about a difference rather than
	about a state.

	The two inputs carry both `data-model` and `name`. The first is what re-renders
	this markup as somebody types; the second is what the plain POST below sends.
	Nothing here writes anything — the save is a route on FieldController, where
	the ROLE_ADMIN guard is visible to a test that reads routes (see the component
	class, and RecordForm for the case that goes the other way).

	**Two states, one control** (XIV-91). A field that is already numbered posts
	straight to the save, because everything it changes is reversible by typing
	something else. A field that is not posts to a *confirmation*, because turning
	numbering on writes numbers into records that already exist and cannot be
	undone — so the button on this card stops being "Save" and becomes "see what
	this would do", and the writing happens on the page after somebody has read
	the answer.
#}
<div{{ attributes.defaults({class: 'card'}) }}>
	<form method="post" action="{{ this.numbered
		? path('field_numbering_save', {module: this.module, field: this.fieldId})
		: path('field_numbering_start', {module: this.module, field: this.fieldId}) }}">
		<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('edit-fields') }}">

		<div class="card-body">
			<div class="row g-3">
				<div class="col-md-7">
					<label class="form-label" for="numbering-pattern">{{ 'field.numbering.pattern'|trans }}</label>
					<input id="numbering-pattern" class="form-control font-monospace" name="sequence"
					       data-model="pattern" value="{{ this.pattern }}" autocomplete="off">
					<p class="form-text mb-0">{{ 'field.numbering.pattern_help'|trans|raw }}</p>
				</div>

				<div class="col-md-5">
					{#
						What it will produce, which is the control's whole reason for
						being live. A pattern that numbers nothing gets a sentence
						instead of a blank space where a number was — the engine's own
						answer to such a pattern is silence (the field goes on being an
						ordinary text field), and silence is the one thing a person
						typing into this box must not be given.
					#}
					{% if this.preview is null %}
						<div class="alert alert-warning mb-0" role="alert">
							{{ 'field.numbering.numbers_nothing'|trans|raw }}
						</div>
					{% else %}
						<div class="border rounded p-3 h-100">
							<div class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.numbering.next_is'|trans }}</div>
							<div class="fs-4 font-monospace" data-numbering-preview>{{ this.preview }}</div>
							<div class="small text-body-secondary mt-1">
								{% if this.period == '' %}
									{{ 'field.numbering.from_forever'|trans }}
								{% else %}
									{{ 'field.numbering.from_year'|trans({'%period%': this.period}) }}
								{% endif %}
							</div>
						</div>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
			</div>

			{#
				The surprise, said out loud before it happens. Switching between a
				pattern with {year} and one without does not renumber anything and does
				not reset anything either — it starts drawing from a counter that has
				always been there and has never been used, which is why the next number
				can be lower than the last one.
			#}
			{% if this.changingCounter %}
				<div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0" role="alert">
					{{ 'field.numbering.counter_switch'|trans({'%at%': this.counterAt}) }}
				</div>
			{% endif %}

			{#
				What this would do to the records that are already here (XIV-91), and
				it is the part of the page that is about data rather than about a
				pattern. Shown only where numbering is being taken up, because that is
				the only moment it is a question: once a field is numbered every new
				record gets its number on the way in and there is nothing to catch up.

				Three sentences, and each of them answers something somebody would
				otherwise find out afterwards — that the empty rows get numbers now
				rather than one at a time as people happen to open them, that the rows
				with something in them keep it, and that a number they typed in
				themselves will not be handed out again. The figures behind the first
				two are counted; the third is checked against the column on the next
				page, where the pattern has stopped changing.
			#}
			{% if not this.numbered %}
				<div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-0" role="alert">
					<p class="mb-1">
						{% if this.blank == 0 %}
							{{ 'field.numbering.on_none_blank'|trans }}
						{% else %}
							{{ 'field.numbering.on_blank'|trans({'%count%': this.blank})|raw }}
						{% endif %}
					</p>
					{% if this.held > 0 %}
						<p class="mb-1">{{ 'field.numbering.on_held'|trans({'%count%': this.held})|raw }}</p>
					{% endif %}
					<p class="mb-0 small">{{ 'field.numbering.on_derived'|trans }}</p>
				</div>
			{% endif %}

			{#
				The counter box belongs to a numbering that exists. Where one is being
				taken up there is nothing to wind forward *from* — and the migration
				case it was built for is already answered better here, by reading the
				numbers the customer typed in themselves and starting above the highest
				of them. Once numbering is on, this page comes back with the control.
			#}
			{% if this.numbered %}
			<hr>

			<div class="row g-3 align-items-start">
				<div class="col-md-5">
					<label class="form-label" for="numbering-next">{{ 'field.numbering.counter_label'|trans }}</label>
					<input id="numbering-next" type="number" min="1" step="1" class="form-control"
					       name="next_value" data-model="nextValue" value="{{ this.nextValue }}"
					       placeholder="{{ this.counterAt }}" autocomplete="off">
					<p class="form-text mb-0">{{ 'field.numbering.counter_help'|trans({'%at%': this.counterAt}) }}</p>
				</div>

				<div class="col-md-7">
					{#
						A courtesy and never the control. `NumberAllocator::restartAt()`
						refuses this in a single statement against the counter's own row,
						so a form posted around this page meets the same rule — see the
						allocator for why the check cannot live in PHP either side of a
						read. What this saves is somebody finding out by being bounced
						back with a flash.
					#}
					{% if this.counterGoingBack %}
						<div class="alert alert-danger mb-0" role="alert">
							{{ 'field.numbering.counter_back'|trans({'%at%': this.counterAt}) }}
						</div>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
			</div>
			{% endif %}

			{# A promise about numbers that have been given out, so it is only true
			   where some have. On a field taking numbering up the honest version of
			   this paragraph is the warning above it, which says the opposite. #}
			{% if this.numbered %}
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mt-3 mb-0">{{ 'field.numbering.nothing_renumbered'|trans }}</p>
			{% endif %}
		</div>

		<div class="card-footer d-flex gap-2">
			{# Disabled on a pattern the editor would refuse anyway: the refusal is on
			   the write path and this is only the door being shut where the reason is
			   already on screen a few centimetres above.

			   The word on it is the difference between the two states: one saves, the
			   other asks to be shown what would happen. A button labelled "Save" in
			   front of a backfill would be the whole problem in one word. #}
			<button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit" {{ this.preview is null ? 'disabled' : '' }}>
				{{ (this.numbered ? 'common.save' : 'field.numbering.review')|trans }}
			</button>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('field_edit', {module: this.module, field: this.fieldId}) }}">
				{{ 'common.cancel'|trans }}
			</a>
		</div>
	</form>
</div>
